  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-72h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On the morning of June 6, Iran launched multiple drones toward the Strait of Hormuz. US CENTCOM stated the drones posed an "immediate threat to regional maritime traffic." US forces shot down four of the drones and struck Iranian coastal surveillance radar installations on Qeshm Island and at Goruk in response (NBC News, Washington Post, Times of Israel). Iran's Mehr news agency described the launches as "warning shots near the strait" most likely linked to US ship movements. No commercial vessel was struck in the exchange. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>On June 4, Israeli and Lebanese officials agreed to a conditional ceasefire framework after US-led talks in Washington (Euronews, Al Jazeera). The framework required Hezbollah to cease fire and withdraw fighters from southern Lebanon "pilot zones." Hezbollah leader Naim Kassem rejected the framework outright on June 4–5, calling the withdrawal demand "surrender, defeat and achieving the enemy's goals," and cross-border fighting continued. This leaves intact the Lebanese front condition Iran named as its precondition for resuming talks with the United States. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">N1</span></p>

      <p>Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi told Al-Mayadeen TV on June 4 that "there is currently no formal process of negotiations between Iran and the United States, but messages are still being exchanged" via mediators (Bloomberg, CBS News). Trump on June 5 told reporters talks were continuing; one unnamed regional source told CNN the talks were "back on track." The contradiction between Araghchi's public statement and US characterizations is unresolved, and no named Iranian or mediator official confirmed a formal resumption of negotiations. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>AIS trackers and IMF PortWatch recorded approximately 10 commercial transits per day through the Strait of Hormuz as of June 5 — 11% of the pre-crisis 95/day baseline. The June 6 drone incident generated no confirmed change in this figure; no carrier announced a return to Hormuz routing and Lloyd's war-risk premiums remained elevated. Four days remain before the June 10 horizon on <span class="cite">F3</span>. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>The Knesset dissolution bill remained in committee deliberation as of June 6. No second reading has been scheduled. The House Committee set a date range of September 8 to October 20 following the 106-0 first-reading vote on June 2, but coalition disagreement between Haredi parties (who prefer September) and Netanyahu (who prefers October) has not produced a specific date. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP (computed summary) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:38%;">Escalation 38%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:42%;">Protracted 42%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:20%;">De-esc 20%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> Iran's June 6 drone launch toward commercial shipping and escalating IRGC actions in the strait (F1: 55%) combine with Hezbollah's ceasefire rejection (N1: 15% acceptance) to maintain a live escalation corridor; a successful IRGC strike on a commercial vessel in the next 72 hours would be the clearest trigger.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> The dominant scenario: message channels remain open (Araghchi confirmed this June 4) but formal negotiations are suspended, Hormuz transits remain at ~10/day, and the Lebanon front continues without resolution, keeping all parties locked in a costly standoff through at least mid-June.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Hinges on Hezbollah accepting a modified ceasefire framework (N1) that allows Iran to declare Lebanon de-escalated and resume formal US-Iran talks (F4: 40%), which is the necessary precondition for any Hormuz reopening (F5: 25%); the path exists but requires sequential events that have not yet begun.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media ran two concurrent frames in the June 4–6 window. The first framed Iran as a sovereign actor exercising rights over the Strait of Hormuz: Fars News disputed Trump's characterization of progress, reporting that the strait "will remain under Iranian control according to the latest version of the proposal exchanged between the US and Iran," and that the United States "has nothing to do with" any Hormuz mechanism, which Iran said must be agreed between Iran, Oman, and Gulf neighbors. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>The second frame concerned the Lebanon front. Tasnim and IRNA ran Araghchi's Al-Mayadeen statement verbatim — "no formal process of negotiations... but messages still exchanged" — paired with his warning that a Beirut strike would trigger "full-scale resumption" of the US-Iran conflict. Mehr news agency described the June 6 drone launch as "warning shots," not an escalation, framing the incident as a defensive maritime-sovereignty action. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span> without move</p>

      <p>Kayhan and hardline commentary portrayed the suspension of formal talks as a position of strength, not rupture, and carried no statement from the Supreme Leader's office on negotiations — consistent with Mojtaba Khamenei's pattern of withholding public endorsement from the diplomatic track. No Iranian principal endorsed or denied the June 6 drone launch as a new closure action. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

